==Role in double-strand break repair==

Double-strand break (DSB) repair by [[homologous recombination]] is initiated by 5' to 3' strand resection ([[DNA end resection|DSB resection]]). In humans, [[DNA2L|DNA2]] nuclease resects the 5'-to-3' strand at the DSB to generate a 3' single-strand DNA (ssDNA) overhang.<ref name="Hoa_2015">{{cite journal | vauthors = Hoa NN, Akagawa R, Yamasaki T, Hirota K, Sasa K, Natsume T, Kobayashi J, Sakuma T, Yamamoto T, Komatsu K, Kanemaki MT, Pommier Y, Takeda S, Sasanuma H | title = Relative contribution of four nucleases, CtIP, Dna2, Exo1 and Mre11, to the initial step of DNA double-strand break repair by homologous recombination in both the chicken DT40 and human TK6 cell lines | journal = Genes to Cells: Devoted to Molecular & Cellular Mechanisms | volume = 20 | issue = 12 | pages = 1059–1076 | date = December 2015 | pmid = 26525166 | pmc = 7747012 | doi = 10.1111/gtc.12310 | doi-access = free }}</ref><ref name="Hoa_2015a">{{cite journal | vauthors = Hoa NN, Kobayashi J, Omura M, Hirakawa M, Yang SH, Komatsu K, Paull TT, Takeda S, Sasanuma H | title = BRCA1 and CtIP Are Both Required to Recruit Dna2 at Double-Strand Breaks in Homologous Recombination | journal = PLOS ONE | volume = 10 | issue = 4 | article-number = e0124495 | year = 2015 | pmid = 25909997 | pmc = 4409214 | doi = 10.1371/journal.pone.0124495 | bibcode = 2015PLoSO..1024495H | doi-access = free }}</ref>

In vertebrates, a number of RAD51 [[Homology (biology)#Paralogy|paralogs]] (see Figure) are essential for RAD51 protein recruitment or stabilization at sites of DNA damage.

[[File:HR proteins conserved domains.svg| 325px | thumb | alt=Graphic showing proteins from each domain of life. Each protein is shown horizontally, with homologous domains on each protein indicated by color. |[[Protein domains]] in homologous recombination-related proteins are conserved across the [[Three-domain system|three main groups]] of life: archaea, bacteria and eukaryotes.]]

In vertebrates and plants, five paralogs of RAD51 are expressed in somatic cells, including RAD51B ([[RAD51L1]]), [[RAD51C]] (RAD51L2), RAD51D ([[RAD51L3]]), [[XRCC2]] and [[XRCC3]].  They each share about 25% amino acid sequence identity with RAD51 and with each other.<ref name="Miller_2004">{{cite journal | vauthors = Miller KA, Sawicka D, Barsky D, Albala JS | title = Domain mapping of the Rad51 paralog protein complexes | journal = Nucleic Acids Research | volume = 32 | issue = 1 | pages = 169–178 | year = 2004 | pmid = 14704354 | pmc = 373258 | doi = 10.1093/nar/gkg925 }}</ref>

Outside of plants and vertebrates, a much broader diversity of Rad51 recombinase paralog proteins exist. In budding yeast ([[Saccharomyces cerevisiae]]), the paralogs Rad55 and Rad57 form a heterodimer that associates with the SHU complex to assemble the six-subunit RRS complex, which recruits Rad51 to ssDNA and promotes Rad51 filament nucleation. The recombinase paralog rfs-1 is found in the round worm [[Caenorhabditis elegans]], however it is not essential for homologous recombination. Among [[archaea]], RadB and RadC recombinase paralogs are found in many organisms belonging to [[Euryarchaeota]], while a broader diversity of related recombinase paralogs are found in [[Crenarchaea]], including Ral1, Ral2, Ral3, RadC, RadC1, and RadC2.

The RAD51 paralogs contribute to efficient DNA double-strand break repair by [[homologous recombination]]. Consequently, experimental depletion of these paralogs often result in significantly reduced homologous recombination.<ref name="Chun_2013">{{cite journal | vauthors = Chun J, Buechelmaier ES, Powell SN | title = Rad51 paralog complexes BCDX2 and CX3 act at different stages in the BRCA1-BRCA2-dependent homologous recombination pathway | journal = Molecular and Cellular Biology | volume = 33 | issue = 2 | pages = 387–395 | date = January 2013 | pmid = 23149936 | pmc = 3554112 | doi = 10.1128/MCB.00465-12 }}</ref>

The paralogs form two identified complexes: BCDX2 (RAD51B-RAD51C-RAD51D-XRCC2) and CX3 (RAD51C-XRCC3). These two complexes act at two different stages of homologous recombinational [[DNA repair]].  The BCDX2 complex is responsible for RAD51 recruitment or stabilization at damage sites.<ref name="Chun_2013" />  The BCDX2 complex appears to act by facilitating the assembly or stability of the [[#Function|RAD51 nucleoprotein filament]].  The CX3 complex acts downstream of RAD51 recruitment to damage sites.<ref name="Chun_2013" />

Another complex, the [[BRCA1]]-[[PALB2]]-[[BRCA2]] complex, cooperates with the RAD51 paralogs to load RAD51 onto ssDNA coated with [[Replication protein A|RPA]] to form the essential recombination intermediate, the RAD51-ssDNA filament.<ref name="Prakash_2015">{{cite journal | vauthors = Prakash R, Zhang Y, Feng W, Jasin M | title = Homologous recombination and human health: the roles of BRCA1, BRCA2, and associated proteins | journal = Cold Spring Harbor Perspectives in Biology | volume = 7 | issue = 4 | article-number = a016600 | date = April 2015 | pmid = 25833843 | pmc = 4382744 | doi = 10.1101/cshperspect.a016600 }}</ref>

In mice and humans, the [[BRCA2]] complex primarily mediates orderly assembly of RAD51 on ssDNA, which is an active substrate in homologous pairing and strand invasion.<ref name="Holloman_2011">{{cite journal | vauthors = Holloman WK | title = Unraveling the mechanism of BRCA2 in homologous recombination | journal = Nature Structural & Molecular Biology | volume = 18 | issue = 7 | pages = 748–754 | date = July 2011 | pmid = 21731065 | pmc = 3647347 | doi = 10.1038/nsmb.2096 }}</ref> BRCA2 also redirects RAD51 from dsDNA and prevents its dissociation from ssDNA.<ref name="Holloman_2011" />  However, in the presence of a BRCA2 mutation, human [[RAD52]] can mediate RAD51 assembly on ssDNA and substitute for BRCA2 in homologous recombinational DNA repair,<ref name="Feng_2011">{{cite journal | vauthors = Feng Z, Scott SP, Bussen W, Sharma GG, Guo G, Pandita TK, Powell SN | title = Rad52 inactivation is synthetically lethal with BRCA2 deficiency | journal = Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America | volume = 108 | issue = 2 | pages = 686–691 | date = January 2011 | pmid = 21148102 | pmc = 3021033 | doi = 10.1073/pnas.1010959107 | doi-access = free }}</ref> though with lower efficiency than BRCA2.

Further steps are detailed in the article [[Homologous recombination#Models|Homologous recombination]].

==Meiosis==

Rad51 has a crucial function in meiotic prophase in mice, with Rad51 knockout leading to a depletion of late prophase I [[spermatocyte]]s.<ref name="Dai_2017">{{cite journal | vauthors = Dai J, Voloshin O, Potapova S, Camerini-Otero RD | title = Meiotic Knockdown and Complementation Reveals Essential Role of RAD51 in Mouse Spermatogenesis | journal = Cell Reports | volume = 18 | issue = 6 | pages = 1383–1394 | date = February 2017 | pmid = 28178517 | pmc = 5358547 | doi = 10.1016/j.celrep.2017.01.024 }}</ref>

During [[meiosis]], the two recombinases, Rad51 and [[DMC1 (gene)|Dmc1]], interact with single-stranded [[DNA]] to form specialized filaments that are adapted for facilitating [[Homologous recombination|recombination]] between [[homologous chromosome]]s.  Both Rad51 and Dmc1 have an intrinsic ability to self-aggregate.<ref name="Crickard_2018">{{cite journal | vauthors = Crickard JB, Kaniecki K, Kwon Y, Sung P, Greene EC | title = Spontaneous self-segregation of Rad51 and Dmc1 DNA recombinases within mixed recombinase filaments | journal = The Journal of Biological Chemistry | volume = 293 | issue = 11 | pages = 4191–4200 | date = March 2018 | pmid = 29382724 | pmc = 5858004 | doi = 10.1074/jbc.RA117.001143 | doi-access = free }}</ref> The presence of Dmc1 stabilizes the adjacent Rad51 filaments suggesting that cross-talk between these two recombinases may affect their biochemical properties.

==Chemotherapy and aging==

In aged and [[chemotherapy]] treated females, oocytes and follicles are depleted by [[apoptosis]] (programmed cell death) leading to [[ovary|ovarian]] failure. DNA damage-induced [[oocyte]] apoptosis depends on the efficiency of the [[DNA repair]] machinery that in turn declines with age.  Survival of oocytes following chemotherapy or aging can be enhanced by increased expression of Rad51.<ref name="Kujjo_2010">{{cite journal | vauthors = Kujjo LL, Laine T, Pereira RJ, Kagawa W, Kurumizaka H, Yokoyama S, Perez GI | title = Enhancing survival of mouse oocytes following chemotherapy or aging by targeting Bax and Rad51 | journal = PLOS ONE | volume = 5 | issue = 2 | article-number = e9204 | date = February 2010 | pmid = 20169201 | pmc = 2820548 | doi = 10.1371/journal.pone.0009204 | bibcode = 2010PLoSO...5.9204K | doi-access = free }}</ref>  The Rad51-induced oocyte resistance to apoptosis is likely due to Rad51's central role in [[homologous recombination]]al repair of DNA damage.

==MicroRNA control of ''RAD51'' expression==

In mammals, [[microRNA]]s (miRNAs) regulate about 60% of the [[gene transcription|transcriptional]] activity of protein-encoding genes.<ref name="Friedman_2009">{{cite journal | vauthors = Friedman RC, Farh KK, Burge CB, Bartel DP | title = Most mammalian mRNAs are conserved targets of microRNAs | journal = Genome Research | volume = 19 | issue = 1 | pages = 92–105 | date = January 2009 | pmid = 18955434 | pmc = 2612969 | doi = 10.1101/gr.082701.108 }}</ref>  Some miRNAs also undergo methylation-associated silencing in cancer cells.<ref name="Saito_2006">{{cite journal | vauthors = Saito Y, Liang G, Egger G, Friedman JM, Chuang JC, Coetzee GA, Jones PA | title = Specific activation of microRNA-127 with downregulation of the proto-oncogene BCL6 by chromatin-modifying drugs in human cancer cells | journal = Cancer Cell | volume = 9 | issue = 6 | pages = 435–443 | date = June 2006 | pmid = 16766263 | doi = 10.1016/j.ccr.2006.04.020 | doi-access = free }}</ref><ref name="Lujambio_2007">{{cite journal | vauthors = Lujambio A, Ropero S, Ballestar E, Fraga MF, Cerrato C, Setién F, Casado S, Suarez-Gauthier A, Sanchez-Cespedes M, Git A, Gitt A, Spiteri I, Das PP, Caldas C, Miska E, Esteller M | title = Genetic unmasking of an epigenetically silenced microRNA in human cancer cells | journal = Cancer Research | volume = 67 | issue = 4 | pages = 1424–1429 | date = February 2007 | pmid = 17308079 | doi = 10.1158/0008-5472.CAN-06-4218 | doi-access = free }}</ref>  If a repressive miRNA is silenced by hypermethylation or deletion, then the gene it typically targets becomes over-expressed.

At least eight miRNAs repressing ''RAD51'' expression have been identified, with five of these appearing significant in cancer. For instance, in [[triple-negative breast cancer]]s (TNBC), ''over-expression'' of miR-155 was reported to coincide with ''RAD51'' repression.<ref name="Gasparini_2014">{{cite journal | vauthors = Gasparini P, Lovat F, Fassan M, Casadei L, Cascione L, Jacob NK, Carasi S, Palmieri D, Costinean S, Shapiro CL, Huebner K, Croce CM | title = Protective role of miR-155 in breast cancer through RAD51 targeting impairs homologous recombination after irradiation | journal = Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America | volume = 111 | issue = 12 | pages = 4536–4541 | date = March 2014 | pmid = 24616504 | pmc = 3970505 | doi = 10.1073/pnas.1402604111 | bibcode = 2014PNAS..111.4536G | doi-access = free }}{{Erratum|doi=10.1073/pnas.1700727114|pmid=28242688|http://retractionwatch.com/2017/03/08/cancer-researcher-dodged-accusations-decades-new-correction/ ''Retraction Watch''|http://retractionwatch.com/?s=carlo+croce ''Retraction Watch''|checked=yes}}</ref>  Further study demonstrated that transfecting breast cancer cells with a vector over-expressing miR-155 represses ''RAD51'' expression, resulting in decreased homologous recombination and increased sensitivity to ionizing radiation.<ref name="Gasparini_2014" />

Four further miRNAs that repress ''RAD51'' (miR-148b* and miR-193b*,<ref name="Choi_2014">{{cite journal | vauthors = Choi YE, Pan Y, Park E, Konstantinopoulos P, De S, D'Andrea A, Chowdhury D | title = MicroRNAs down-regulate homologous recombination in the G1 phase of cycling cells to maintain genomic stability | journal = eLife | volume = 3 | article-number = e02445 | date = April 2014 | pmid = 24843000 | pmc = 4031983 | doi = 10.7554/eLife.02445 | doi-access = free }}</ref> miR-506,<ref name="Liu_2015">{{cite journal | vauthors = Liu G, Xue F, Zhang W | title = miR-506: a regulator of chemo-sensitivity through suppression of the RAD51-homologous recombination axis | journal = Chinese Journal of Cancer | volume = 34 | issue = 11 | pages = 485–487 | date = September 2015 | article-number = 44 | pmid = 26369335 | pmc = 4593343 | doi = 10.1186/s40880-015-0049-z | doi-access = free }}</ref> and miR-34a<ref name="Cortez_2015">{{cite journal | vauthors = Cortez MA, Valdecanas D, Niknam S, Peltier HJ, Diao L, Giri U, Komaki R, Calin GA, Gomez DR, Chang JY, Heymach JV, Bader AG, Welsh JW | title = In Vivo Delivery of miR-34a Sensitizes Lung Tumors to Radiation Through RAD51 Regulation | journal = Molecular Therapy. Nucleic Acids | volume = 4 | issue = 12 | article-number = e270 | date = December 2015 | pmid = 26670277 | pmc = 5014539 | doi = 10.1038/mtna.2015.47 }}</ref>) are ''under-expressed'' in cancers, presumably leading to induction of ''RAD51'' expression, with under-expression of miR-148b and miR-193b causing an observed induction of ''RAD51'' expression.<ref name="Choi_2014" /> Furthermore, deletions of miR-148b* and miR-193b* in serous [[ovarian tumor]]s correlates with increased [[Loss of heterozygosity|losses of heterozygosity]] (LOH), which may be carcinogenic. This is thought to arise from increased ''RAD51'' expression that can drive excess recombination.<ref name="Choi_2014" />

Under-expression of miR-506 is associated with both faster cancer recurrence and reduced survival in [[Surface epithelial-stromal tumor|epithelial ovarian cancer]] patients.<ref name="Liu_2015a">{{cite journal | vauthors = Liu G, Yang D, Rupaimoole R, Pecot CV, Sun Y, Mangala LS, Li X, Ji P, Cogdell D, Hu L, Wang Y, Rodriguez-Aguayo C, Lopez-Berestein G, Shmulevich I, De Cecco L, Chen K, Mezzanzanica D, Xue F, Sood AK, Zhang W | title = Augmentation of response to chemotherapy by microRNA-506 through regulation of RAD51 in serous ovarian cancers | journal = Journal of the National Cancer Institute | volume = 107 | issue = 7 | article-number = djv108 | date = July 2015 | pmid = 25995442 | pmc = 4554255 | doi = 10.1093/jnci/djv108 }}</ref>

Methylation of the promoter of miR-34a, which leads to miR-34a under-expression, is observed in 79% of [[prostate cancer]]s and 63% of primary [[melanoma]]s.<ref name="Lodygin_2008">{{cite journal | vauthors = Lodygin D, Tarasov V, Epanchintsev A, Berking C, Knyazeva T, Körner H, Knyazev P, Diebold J, Hermeking H | title = Inactivation of miR-34a by aberrant CpG methylation in multiple types of cancer | journal = Cell Cycle | location = Georgetown, Tex. | volume = 7 | issue = 16 | pages = 2591–2600 | date = August 2008 | pmid = 18719384 | doi = 10.4161/cc.7.16.6533 | doi-access = free }}</ref>  Reduced miR-34a expression has also been observed in 63% of [[Non-small-cell lung cancer|non-small cell lung cancers]],<ref name="Wiggins_2010">{{cite journal | vauthors = Wiggins JF, Ruffino L, Kelnar K, Omotola M, Patrawala L, Brown D, Bader AG | title = Development of a lung cancer therapeutic based on the tumor suppressor microRNA-34 | journal = Cancer Research | volume = 70 | issue = 14 | pages = 5923–5930 | date = July 2010 | pmid = 20570894 | pmc = 2913706 | doi = 10.1158/0008-5472.CAN-10-0655 }}</ref> and 36% of [[Colorectal cancer|colon cancers]],<ref name="Tazawa_2007">{{cite journal | vauthors = Tazawa H, Tsuchiya N, Izumiya M, Nakagama H | title = Tumor-suppressive miR-34a induces senescence-like growth arrest through modulation of the E2F pathway in human colon cancer cells | journal = Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America | volume = 104 | issue = 39 | pages = 15472–15477 | date = September 2007 | pmid = 17875987 | pmc = 2000550 | doi = 10.1073/pnas.0707351104 | bibcode = 2007PNAS..10415472T | doi-access = free }}</ref> and is generally also under-expressed in primary [[neuroblastoma]]s.<ref name="Welch_2007">{{cite journal | vauthors = Welch C, Chen Y, Stallings RL | title = MicroRNA-34a functions as a potential tumor suppressor by inducing apoptosis in neuroblastoma cells | journal = Oncogene | volume = 26 | issue = 34 | pages = 5017–5022 | date = July 2007 | pmid = 17297439 | doi = 10.1038/sj.onc.1210293 | doi-access = free }}</ref>

