Three other microRNAs have been identified, by various criteria, as likely to repress ''RAD51'' (miR-96,<ref name="Wang_2012">{{cite journal | vauthors = Wang Y, Huang JW, Calses P, Kemp CJ, Taniguchi T | title = MiR-96 downregulates REV1 and RAD51 to promote cellular sensitivity to cisplatin and PARP inhibition | journal = Cancer Research | volume = 72 | issue = 16 | pages = 4037–4046 | date = August 2012 | pmid = 22761336 | pmc = 3421071 | doi = 10.1158/0008-5472.CAN-12-0103 }}</ref> miR-203,<ref name="Chang_2016">{{cite journal | vauthors = Chang JH, Hwang YH, Lee DJ, Kim DH, Park JM, Wu HG, Kim IA | title = MicroRNA-203 Modulates the Radiation Sensitivity of Human Malignant Glioma Cells | journal = International Journal of Radiation Oncology, Biology, Physics | volume = 94 | issue = 2 | pages = 412–420 | date = February 2016 | pmid = 26678661 | doi = 10.1016/j.ijrobp.2015.10.001 }}</ref> and miR-103/107<ref name="Huang_2013">{{cite journal | vauthors = Huang JW, Wang Y, Dhillon KK, Calses P, Villegas E, Mitchell PS, Tewari M, Kemp CJ, Taniguchi T | title = Systematic screen identifies miRNAs that target RAD51 and RAD51D to enhance chemosensitivity | journal = Molecular Cancer Research  | volume = 11 | issue = 12 | pages = 1564–1573 | date = December 2013 | pmid = 24088786 | pmc = 3869885 | doi = 10.1158/1541-7786.MCR-13-0292 }}</ref>).  These microRNAs were then tested by over-expressing them in cells ''in vitro'', and they were found to indeed repress ''RAD51''.  This repression was generally associated with decreased HR and increased sensitivity of the cells to DNA damaging agents.

== Pathology ==

=== Cancer ===
This protein is also found to interact with [[PALB2]]<ref name="Buisson_2010" /> and [[BRCA2]], which may be important for the cellular response to DNA damage. BRCA2 is shown to regulate both the intracellular localization and DNA-binding ability of this protein. Loss of these controls following BRCA2 inactivation may be a key event leading to genomic instability and tumorigenesis.<ref>{{cite journal | vauthors = Daniel DC | title = Highlight: BRCA1 and BRCA2 proteins in breast cancer | journal = Microscopy Research and Technique | volume = 59 | issue = 1 | pages = 68–83 | date = October 2002 | pmid = 12242698 | doi = 10.1002/jemt.10178 | s2cid = 30091586 | doi-access = free }}</ref>

Several alterations of the Rad51  gene have been associated with an increased risk of developing [[breast cancer]]. The breast cancer susceptibility protein [[BRCA2]] and [[PALB2]] controls the function of Rad51 in the pathway for DNA repair by homologous recombination.<ref name="Buisson_2010" /><ref name="Pellegrini_2002">{{cite journal | vauthors = Pellegrini L, Yu DS, Lo T, Anand S, Lee M, Blundell TL, Venkitaraman AR | title = Insights into DNA recombination from the structure of a RAD51-BRCA2 complex | journal = Nature | volume = 420 | issue = 6913 | pages = 287–293 | date = November 2002 | pmid = 12442171 | doi = 10.1038/nature01230 | bibcode = 2002Natur.420..287P | s2cid = 4359383 }}</ref>
In addition to the data listed in Table 1, increased RAD51 expression levels have been identified in metastatic canine mammary carcinoma, indicating that genomic instability plays an important role in the carcinogenesis of this tumor type.<ref>{{cite journal | vauthors = Klopfleisch R, von Euler H, Sarli G, Pinho SS, Gärtner F, Gruber AD | title = Molecular carcinogenesis of canine mammary tumors: news from an old disease | journal = Veterinary Pathology | volume = 48 | issue = 1 | pages = 98–116 | date = January 2011 | pmid = 21149845 | doi = 10.1177/0300985810390826 | s2cid = 206509356 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal | vauthors = Klopfleisch R, Gruber AD | title = Increased expression of BRCA2 and RAD51 in lymph node metastases of canine mammary adenocarcinomas | journal = Veterinary Pathology | volume = 46 | issue = 3 | pages = 416–422 | date = May 2009 | pmid = 19176491 | doi = 10.1354/vp.08-VP-0212-K-FL | s2cid = 11583190 | doi-access = free }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal | vauthors = Klopfleisch R, Schütze M, Gruber AD | title = RAD51 protein expression is increased in canine mammary carcinomas | journal = Veterinary Pathology | volume = 47 | issue = 1 | pages = 98–101 | date = January 2010 | pmid = 20080488 | doi = 10.1177/0300985809353310 | s2cid = 37774507 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal | vauthors = Klopfleisch R, Klose P, Gruber AD | title = The combined expression pattern of BMP2, LTBP4, and DERL1 discriminates malignant from benign canine mammary tumors | journal = Veterinary Pathology | volume = 47 | issue = 3 | pages = 446–454 | date = May 2010 | pmid = 20375427 | doi = 10.1177/0300985810363904 | s2cid = 24379106 }}</ref>

=== Fanconi anemia ===
[[Fanconi anemia]] (FA) is a hereditary condition characterized by cellular hypersensitivity to DNA cross-linking agents.  A dominant negative [[mutation]] in the Rad51 gene has been reported to give rise to an FA-like [[phenotype]] with features of intellectual disability.<ref name="Wang_2015">{{cite journal | vauthors = Wang AT, Kim T, Wagner JE, Conti BA, Lach FP, Huang AL, Molina H, Sanborn EM, Zierhut H, Cornes BK, Abhyankar A, Sougnez C, Gabriel SB, Auerbach AD, Kowalczykowski SC, Smogorzewska A | title = A Dominant Mutation in Human RAD51 Reveals Its Function in DNA Interstrand Crosslink Repair Independent of Homologous Recombination | journal = Molecular Cell | volume = 59 | issue = 3 | pages = 478–490 | date = August 2015 | pmid = 26253028 | pmc = 4529964 | doi = 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.07.009 }}</ref><ref name="Ameziane_2015">{{cite journal | vauthors = Ameziane N, May P, Haitjema A, van de Vrugt HJ, van Rossum-Fikkert SE, Ristic D, Williams GJ, Balk J, Rockx D, Li H, Rooimans MA, Oostra AB, Velleuer E, Dietrich R, Bleijerveld OB, Maarten Altelaar AF, Meijers-Heijboer H, Joenje H, Glusman G, Roach J, Hood L, Galas D, Wyman C, Balling R, den Dunnen J, de Winter JP, Kanaar R, Gelinas R, Dorsman JC | title = A novel Fanconi anaemia subtype associated with a dominant-negative mutation in RAD51 | journal = Nature Communications | volume = 6 | article-number = 8829 | date = December 2015 | pmid = 26681308 | pmc = 4703882 | doi = 10.1038/ncomms9829  | doi-access = free| bibcode = 2015NatCo...6.8829A }}</ref> This report included evidence that Rad51-mediated [[homologous recombination]]al repair likely has an important role in neurodevelopment.

=== Congenital mirror movements ===
Mutations in Rad51 have been linked to both sporadic and familial cases of [[congenital mirror movement disorder]].<ref>{{Cite journal | vauthors = Depienne C, Bouteiller D, Méneret A, Billot S, Groppa S, Klebe S, Charbonnier-Beaupel F, Corvol JC, Saraiva JP, Brueggemann N, Bhatia K, Cincotta M, Brochard V, Flamand-Roze C, Carpentier W | title = RAD51 Haploinsufficiency Causes Congenital Mirror Movements in Humans | journal = The American Journal of Human Genetics | volume = 90 | issue = 2 | pages = 301–307 | date = February 2012 | pmid = 22305526 | pmc = 3276668 | doi = 10.1016/j.ajhg.2011.12.002 | language = en }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal | vauthors = Méneret A, Depienne C, Riant F, Trouillard O, Bouteiller D, Cincotta M, Bitoun P, Wickert J, Lagroua I, Westenberger A, Borgheresi A, Doummar D, Romano M, Rossi S, Defebvre L | title = Congenital mirror movements: Mutational analysis of RAD51 and DCC in 26 cases | journal = Neurology | volume = 82 | issue = 22 | pages = 1999–2002 | date = 2014-06-03 | doi = 10.1212/WNL.0000000000000477 | pmid = 24808016 | pmc = 4105259 | language = en | issn = 0028-3878 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal | vauthors = Méneret A, Depienne C, Riant F, Trouillard O, Bouteiller D, Cincotta M, Bitoun P, Wickert J, Lagroua I, Westenberger A, Borgheresi A, Doummar D, Romano M, Rossi S, Defebvre L | title = Congenital mirror movements | journal = Neurology | volume = 82 | issue = 22 | pages = 1999–2002 | date = 2014-06-03 | doi = 10.1212/WNL.0000000000000477 | pmid = 24808016 | url = https://www.neurology.org/doi/10.1212/WNL.0000000000000477 }}</ref> In neurons, a novel role for Rad51 has been identified in the regulation of [[Netrin]] signalling and axon misguidance phenotypes.<ref>{{Cite journal | vauthors = Glendining KA, Markie D, Gardner RJ, Franz EA, Robertson SP, Jasoni CL | title = A novel role for the DNA repair gene Rad51 in Netrin-1 signalling | journal = Scientific Reports | volume = 7 | issue = 1 | article-number = 39823 | date = 2017-01-06 | pmid = 28057929 | pmc = 5216413 | doi = 10.1038/srep39823  | doi-access = free| bibcode = 2017NatSR...739823G | language = en | issn = 2045-2322 }}</ref>  This role is not clearly linked to its known DNA repair function.

